.TH ID 1 "March 2026" "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
id \- return user identity
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B id
.RB [ \-Z ]
.RI [ user ]
.br
.B id
.RB [ \-G ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-z ]
.RI [ user ]
.br
.B id
.RB [ \-g ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-r ]
.RB [ \-z ]
.RI [ user ]
.br
.B id
.RB [ \-u ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-r ]
.RB [ \-z ]
.RI [ user ]
.br
.B id
.RB [ \-p | \-P | \-c | \-d | \-s | \-A | \-M | \-R ]
.RI [ user ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Print user and group information for the specified
.IR user ,
or (when
.I user
omitted) for the current user.
.PP
.B id
by default prints the real and effective user and group IDs and all 
supplementary groups of the current process, or the specified user.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user
Output only the effective user ID.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-group
Output only the effective group ID.
.TP
.BR \-G ", " \-\-groups
Output all different group IDs (effective, real, and supplementary).
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name
With
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-g ,
or
.BR \-G ,
output the name instead of the numeric ID. If the name cannot be mapped,
the numeric ID is output.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-real
With
.BR \-u
or
.BR \-g ,
output the real ID instead of the effective ID.
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero
Delimit output items with NUL characters instead of spaces or newlines.
This option is not permitted in the default output format.
.TP
.BR \-Z ", " \-\-context
Print only the security context of the process. Exits with status 1
if the system does not support security contexts.
.TP
.B \-p
Make the output human-readable, outputting multiline information categorized
as login, uid, euid, rgid, and groups. (BSD extension)
.TP
.B \-P
Output a passwd(5)-style colon-separated entry. (FreeBSD extension)
.TP
.B \-c
Output the login class. (BSD extension)
.TP
.B \-d
Output the home directory. (FreeBSD extension)
.TP
.B \-s
Output the login shell. (FreeBSD extension)
.TP
.B \-A
Output the process audit user ID. (FreeBSD extension)
.TP
.B \-M
Output the MAC label of the current process. (FreeBSD extension)
.TP
.B \-R
Output the routing table of the current process. (OpenBSD extension)
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display this help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Display version information and exit.
.SH CONFORMING TO
The
.B id
utility is compliant with the POSIX.1-2024 Shell and Utilities specification,
with extensions derived from GNU coreutils and BSD systems (FreeBSD, OpenBSD, NetBSD).
.SH EXIT STATUS
The
.B id
utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR groups (1),
.BR whoami (1),
.BR getuid (2)
